{"name":"Lake Reflection","hex":"#9dd8db","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lake","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large, landlocked stretch of water or similar liquid.","A large amount of liquid."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An offering, sacrifice, gift.","Play; sport; game; fun; glee."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lake"},{"term":"reflection","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of reflecting or the state of being reflected.","The property of a propagated wave being thrown back from a surface (such as a mirror)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/reflection"}],"entries":[{"title":"Reflection (physics)","blocks":[{"text":"Reflection is the change in direction of a wavefront at an interface between two different media so that the wavefront returns into the medium from which it originated. Common examples include the reflection of light, sound and water waves. The law of reflection says that for specular reflection the angle at which the wave is incident on the surface equals the angle at which it is reflected."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Mount_Hood_reflected_in_Mirror_Lake%2C_Oregon.jpg/330px-Mount_Hood_reflected_in_Mirror_Lake%2C_Oregon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reflection_(physics)","wikidata":"Q165939"},{"title":"Lake","blocks":[{"text":"A lake is often a naturally occurring, relatively large and fixed body of water on or near the Earth's surface. It is localized in a basin or interconnected basins surrounded by dry land. Lakes lie completely on land and are separate from the ocean, although they may be connected with the ocean by outflowing rivers. Lakes, like other bodies of water, are part of the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Most lakes are fresh water and account for almost all the world's surface freshwater, but some are salt lakes with salinities even higher than that of seawater. Lakes vary significantly in surface area and volume of water, but in total cover approximately 2.5 × 106 km2 (less than 2%) of the Earth's surface."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Lake_Idro_Italy_2005-08-16.jpg/330px-Lake_Idro_Italy_2005-08-16.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake","wikidata":"Q23397"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/MIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg/500px-MIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/MIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg/500px-MIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sketches of specular, spread, diffuse, and compound reflection.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg/500px-F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg/1280px-F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg/500px-F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An example of the law of reflection","link":"https://en.wik